@mainpage
The @b µTest++ project is a **lightweight**, **macro-free**, **modern C++**
testing framework **with TAP output**, purpose-built for embedded systems
development.

With its clean and expressive C++ API, µTest++ enables developers to write,
organise, and execute unit tests with minimal overhead. The library
includes advanced features such as **test suites**, **test cases**, and
detailed reporting, all tailored to meet the specific needs of embedded
environments.

Test results are reported in the
[TAP](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Test_Anything_Protocol)
(**Test Anything Protocol**)
format by default, ensuring compatibility with a wide range of
continuous integration systems and TAP-aware test harnesses. A
human-readable reporter is also available for interactive use.

Output verbosity is fully configurable via command-line options, allowing
users to choose between concise summaries and comprehensive diagnostic
information to best suit their workflow.

By adopting µTest++, you can achieve robust and maintainable code through
systematic and automated testing, supporting professional software
engineering practices across your entire project.

@section mainpage-start Getting started

The applications include a single header, `micro-os-plus/micro-test-plus.h`.
The header declares nothing unless the macro
`MICRO_OS_PLUS_MICRO_TEST_PLUS_ENABLED` is defined, preferably in
`micro-os-plus/micro-test-plus-defines.h`, for the application and for
the library sources (see @ref micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-config).

A complete test program has a runner, initialised with the command line
arguments, a top suite created in `main()` (optional, since the suites can
be added to the runner instead), test cases with checks, and returns the
exit code of the session:

@code{.cpp}
#include <micro-os-plus/micro-test-plus.h>

namespace mt = micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus;

mt::runner tr;

int
main (int argc, char* argv[])
{
  tr.initialise (argc, argv);

  auto& ts = tr.add_top_suite ();

  ts.test ("Check truth", [] (auto& t) {
    t.expect (mt::eq (6 * 7, 42)) << "the answer";
  });

  return tr.run_suites ();
}
@endcode

The topics of the @ref micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cpp-api group
describe the runner, the suites, the test cases, and the checks, and the
@ref micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cli group the options of the test
programs. The installation, the first project, and the user manual are
in the
<a href="../getting-started/">Getting Started</a>
and
<a href="../user/">User's Guide</a>
pages of the site.
